walk_release.py — the three controls, 2026-09-17, against release v/20260917T091943Z/

Written by hand around captured output. Each control's rc was written with `echo $? > <file>` on the
line after the process, NOT read through a pipe: the first attempt at this file used
`${PIPESTATUS[0]}`, which zsh does not have (it is `$pipestatus[1]`), and recorded `control-1 rc=` —
an empty exit code beside a page of real failures. That earlier partial capture is replaced here.

A control is a claim that the probe DISCRIMINATES. Without them, `problems: []` is indistinguishable
from a probe that looks at nothing.

====================================================================================================
CONTROL 1 — the same release served WITHOUT `media-src 'self'`

The published CSP is parsed out of platform/infra/lib/site-stack.ts. /tmp/csp_mutant_server.py
(source below) serves the identical site/dist tree on port 8902 under that policy MINUS the
media-src directive, i.e. the CSP this stack shipped before PR #54. Expected: the mp4 and the vtt
both blocked, MediaError code 4 at the element, in both locales, and a non-zero rc.

$ walk_release.py --base http://127.0.0.1:8902 --prefix /v/20260917T091943Z

--- csp_mutant_server.py --------------------------------------------------------------------------
"""Positive control for walk_release.py: the SAME dist, served under the SAME policy MINUS
`media-src 'self'`. If the probe's CSP and video arms are live, the two <video> elements must fail.
The stack is not edited — the policy is parsed from it and one directive is dropped here.
"""
import functools, http.server, socketserver, sys
sys.path.insert(0, "platform/build")
import csp_preview

csp = "; ".join(d for d in csp_preview.csp_from_stack().split("; ") if not d.startswith("media-src"))
assert "media-src" not in csp and "default-src" in csp, csp
print("serving under:", csp, flush=True)
socketserver.TCPServer.allow_reuse_address = True
handler = functools.partial(csp_preview.make_handler(csp), directory="site/dist")
with socketserver.TCPServer(("127.0.0.1", 8902), handler) as httpd:
    httpd.serve_forever()

